Z||000000||Jay||05-08-2000||09:12 PM||||Okay, I've got my Northstar in, I've got all the wiring done, all the coolant and fuel lines done.  All that is left is the axles.  I'm using the Caddilac Tranny but Fiero spindles.  Where do I take my measurements from?  If anyone has done this already, I could use some help with measurements.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||05-08-2000||10:17 PM||||My best advise would be to talk to your local axle rebuilding shop.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jay||05-08-2000||10:41 PM||||Thanks, but I wanted some info so I don't go in there not knowing what I'm talking about. Never had to fiddle with axles, you know what I mean?||||reg||1
Z||000003||coyote||05-10-2000||12:55 PM||||have you mmounted the engine on the craddle yet||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jay||05-10-2000||03:25 PM||||YES, ENGINE IS MOUNTED, WIRING IS DONE,FUEL AND COOLANT LINES DONE.  I THINK THE EASIEST THING TO DO IS CHANGE FROM FIERO SPINDLES AND HUB TO CADDILAC SPINDLES AND HUB.  I'VE BEEN TOLD THAT IS THE EASIEST METHOD.  THE ONLY PROBLEM IS I'LL THEN HAVE DIFFERENT BOLT PATTERNS FRONT FROM BACK.  ADVICE?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dan86gt3.8||05-11-2000||04:22 AM||||jay thats awesome!!!!!  You did all the northstar stuff yourself.  I am a little knowledgable about this but not much.  When we did my axles they had to be a certian length when together and compressed.  But I don't know what the measurement is supposed to be or how about to calculate it.  You might try caling ace or design one.  They may be willing to tell you what they do.  But hey post pics as sppn as you can, also tell us how many vette/ls1 f-bodys guys you send home crying lol  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jay||05-11-2000||08:37 AM||||Thanks Dan86gt3.8.  I've tried ACE and Design One.  I've asked to just buy the axles but they won't do it.  They want me to buy their whole kit for about $10 000 and then I'll get the axels thrown in (yeah right!)  Why spend 10 grand US when I could do the work myself?  Besides I live in Toronto Canada, that $10000 when converted to Canadian Dollars is close to $15 000.  Not to mention the shipping charges!  Hell No!  I did it with some friends, and I've got to tell you, it's not that difficult.  I didn't take any chances though with the wiring, I had that done by Howel Engine Developements in Michigan.  If anyone needs help or wants details for their own Northstar, feel free to e-mail me or check out our club site at <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/gtaclub" TARGET=_blank>http://www.members.home.net/gtaclub</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||HarryG||05-11-2000||05:19 PM||||Jay, <BR>I don't know the method for figuring axle length, but you might look at:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.inetport.com/~van_blok" TARGET=_blank>www.inetport.com/~van_blok</A> <BR>Dale Van Blokland is putting a Northstar into a Diablo re-body.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.gkndrivetech.com" TARGET=_blank>www.gkndrivetech.com</A> <BR>This company makes stock-type axles and also custom stuff.<P>Hope this helps!<BR>Harry<BR>  ||||reg||3
Z||000008||Dan86gt3.8||05-11-2000||05:33 PM||||well I figured one of them would be unselfish enough as to at least give you the lengths.  Ummm you might try "theogre" or terrayk they usually seem to know about more advanced stuff like this.  Umm my only othe idea is maybe check with the import guys that do engine swaps and see how they figure out thier axle stuff, cause they also use the good ole half shaft.  Umm other than that I am all out of ideas you might run a search on cv joint questions I know I posted one  back a couple months ago||||reg||1
Z||000009||Dan86gt3.8||05-11-2000||05:58 PM||||jay check out the "for thoose that have the 3.8....." theogre gave me some pretty good info might help you some, might try shooting him an e-mail.....||||reg||1
Z||000010||Jay||05-11-2000||06:20 PM||||Okay, today I bought a set of Caddilac axles.  From what I have gathered these will fit if I change the spindle to a heavy duty, FWD strut set up.  The only problem is that the axles will be tight, with no room for movement back and forth when turning or going over bumps ect.  I was told to cut 3/8" off both sides of the shaft, then have them re-splined 3/8" deeper to compensate for what was cut off.  Do the measurements need to be exact or is there room for error?  I don't want to ruin $200 worth of axles by cutting too much off.  ||||reg||1
Z||000011||lowCG||05-11-2000||10:07 PM||||Axles need to be able and;<P>1.Not bind  during the travel of the suspension from being too long.(I'd want nearly a .250" of extra float,to make sure)<P>2.Not fall out of the tranny on the other extreme motion of the suspension travel.<P>I'd ask the axle shop that is going to be doing the machine work/balancing,so you're communicating the same distances,and measuring from the same surfaces,etc.  ||||reg||1
